---
// Custom Momence case study. Notion drives metadata; this file owns
// the body. Spine: decompose before you draw. The four-category schema
// is the contract; layout is the implementation. Modularity becomes a
// customer-tailoring feature instead of a usability tax.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import MomenceTenantSwitcher from "../../components/case-study/showcase/MomenceTenantSwitcher.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Momence — Jon Tollefson"
  description="A customer profile that bends to fit each business. 48-hour take-home redesign of Momence's customer profile: four-category schema, modular by tenant, paired desktop and mobile."
  ogImage="/case-studies/momence/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 05 · May 2023 — 48-hour take-home"
    title="Momence"
    subtitle="A customer profile that bends to fit each business."
    cover="/case-studies/momence/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Sole designer · self-initiated case study" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2023 · 48 hours, brief to submission" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Figma" },
      { label: "Audience", value: "Yoga / coaching / wellness admins" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE PITCH ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="Final-stage take-home for the senior product designer interview. Brief: one screenshot of the existing customer profile and a written spec for a yoga studio admin managing customers with active memberships, packs, and sessions. Two days." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The existing UI was dense but flat. Identity data, financial metrics, behavioral history, and configuration all shared the same visual weight on one undifferentiated surface. Reading it surfaced three structural problems: no hierarchy, no tenant flexibility, no mobile counterpart.</p>

      <p>The interesting bet of this redesign was to refuse to draw a layout until the schema was named. Categorization first. Modularity second. Layout third. The component schema became the deliverable that did the heaviest lifting, and the layout downstream of it could shift per tenant without a redesign cycle.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        Decompose before you draw. The schema is the contract. The layout is the implementation.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 48, label: "hours from brief to submission", suffix: "h", color: "white" },
        { n: 4, label: "functional categories in the schema", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 2, label: "form factors paired (web + mobile)", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 1, label: "tenant the brief covered, schema covers many", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE EXISTING UI ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="What the existing UI did wrong" deck="Three structural problems surfaced from a single read of the existing customer detail page. None of them were fixable by 'cleaning up the layout' — they needed a different organizing logic underneath.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/momence/notion-35fed8d4704f805591b2d6d7268dc597.png"
      alt="The existing Momence customer profile UI. Left rail with Yaz Kahrouf's identity and a documents/custom-info-fields block. Main area shows tabs for Overview, Activity, Messages, Notes. Active Subscriptions, Active Packs, and a long Recent Activity feed cascade below. Everything sits at the same visual weight on one flat surface."
      caption="Identity, finance, behavior, and admin configuration shared the same visual hierarchy. Nothing said 'look here first.' A yoga studio admin and a B2B coach surfaced the same priorities, despite running fundamentally different businesses. Mobile didn't exist."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <ul class="generalize three">
      <li>
        <strong>Flat hierarchy.</strong>
        Identity, finance, behavior, and admin configuration shared the same visual weight. Nothing said 'look here first.'
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No tenant flexibility.</strong>
        A yoga studio doesn't surface the same priorities as a B2B coaching practice, but the layout couldn't be tailored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mobile parity gap.</strong>
        A dense desktop layout with no mobile counterpart, despite an audience often running their business from a phone between classes.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · DECOMPOSE BEFORE DRAW ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Decompose before you draw" deck="Four functional categories sit underneath every Momence customer profile. Identifying them before drawing made the layout reshufflable without giving up coherence.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/momence/notion-35fed8d4704f80d2acc0f7e4dfb69326.png"
      alt="The existing Momence customer profile annotated. Four large color-coded labels overlay regions of the UI: 'Client Contact' on the left identity rail, 'Business-Specific' on the active subscriptions and active packs sections, 'User Interactions' on the recent activity feed, and 'Flex / Custom Fields' on the bottom-left custom info area."
      caption="Four functional categories sit underneath every Momence customer profile: Client Contact (identity, comms), Business-Specific (financials, subscriptions, credits), Flex Fields (per-tenant custom configuration), and User Interactions (activity, messages, notes). Same primitives, every tenant."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · TENANT SWITCHER ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="Modularity as a tenant feature" deck="The schema is constant. Priority order, density, and flex-field labels are not. Pick a tenant — watch the same customer profile reshuffle to match the business, without a single net-new component.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · PICK A TENANT"
      caption="Yoga studio leads with Sessions Attended. B2B coaching leads with Hours coached and Contract size. Pilates leads with Reformer sessions and apparatus preference. Wellness leads with Treatments YTD and pressure-pref flex fields. Same Client Contact / Business / Flex / Interactions schema underneath every one."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <MomenceTenantSwitcher />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The reason this works is that none of the tenants need a different set of primitives. They need different priority and different flex. The schema names the four primitives once. The tenant config picks which one leads, the flex fields each tenant defines name themselves, and the layout downstream gets to be opinionated about which KPI sits in the lead card without breaking the contract.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · WEB ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="Web — KPIs lead, supporting context follows" deck="A 1366px customer detail built on draggable modules sitting on top of the four-category schema. Primary KPIs lead with trend indicators. The Recent Activity feed unifies messages, payments, class check-ins, and emails behind one set of macro icons. A collapsible right rail surfaces upcoming events, documents, automations, and tickets.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/momence/notion-35fed8d4704f80e58a8fc06ae0e94090.png"
      alt="The Momence web customer detail page redesign. Left rail: Yaz Kahrouf identity, custom info fields, customer tags. Center column: large KPI cards (Sessions Attended 44 +11%, Total Spend $863.65 +17%), Active Subscriptions chips, Available Credits, Email Subscriptions toggle. Center-right column: a Recent Activity feed with category filters (Everything, Activity, Messages, Notes), date-sorted entries mixing check-ins, payments, and email threads. Right rail: collapsible Upcoming Events calendar, Documents, Automations toggle, Attachments, Tickets — all with view-counts and status dots."
      caption="A snapshot is data. A snapshot with direction is product. Every KPI carries a trend indicator (+11% over 3 months, +17% last month) so the number reads as movement, not just a current value."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/momence/notion-35fed8d4704f802384e5f160d438fb5f.png"
      alt="The Recent Activity panel detailed. Custom macro icons distinguish activity type (orange person for check-in, black dollar for payment, blue email). An email subject line expands inline with the message body when relevant. Annotations: 'Filter content by category or input custom search', 'Integrated email to provide full context of client interactions', 'Custom macro icons to quickly distinguish activity type', 'Easily navigate to full activity page'."
      caption="Activity is the one surface that has to carry messages, payments, check-ins, and email threads at once. Macro icons collapse the type-distinction into a glance; expanded email previews keep the actual message in context."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · MOBILE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="Mobile — density without loss" deck="360px. The same four primitives, collapsed for a phone, with a calendar surface and status indicators leading. A studio admin running their business between classes gets time-sensitive context first, full record one tap below.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/momence/notion-35fed8d4704f80d58efffafb4bad62dd.png"
      alt="The Momence mobile customer detail. Dark hero band with avatar 'YK' and 'Yaz Kahrouf', 'Joined Aug 2022 · Attended today.' Below: mobile/email input fields, three tab buttons (Info, Activity, More). Two KPI cards: $863.65 Total Spend +17% and 44 Sessions Attended +11%. Active Subscriptions chip row, Tags chip row, Available Credits and Email Subscriptions rows. Bottom: Overview / Customers / Support / Settings tab bar."
      caption="Collapsible flex menus preserve density without sacrificing thumb-reach. Status indicators (Attended today, +11%) lead so a studio admin walking back from class scans the same hierarchy as the desktop reader, one tap to drill."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · PRINCIPLES + REFLECTIONS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What generalizes" deck="The two moves I'd repeat on any data-dense admin redesign.">
    <ul class="generalize">
      <li>
        <strong>Decompose before you draw.</strong>
        Naming the four functional categories before any layout work is the move I'd repeat on any data-dense admin redesign. Without it, "drag to rearrange" becomes drag-to-rearrange chaos. With it, modularity becomes a customer-tailoring feature instead of a usability tax.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A snapshot is data. A snapshot with direction is product.</strong>
        Trend indicators on KPI cards move users from "read the number" to "act on the movement." That's where data-dense admin tools earn their keep — turning a state read into an action prompt.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p class="muted">48 hours from brief to submission, working from one screenshot of the existing customer profile and a written spec for a yoga studio admin. Not shipped. All design decisions are mine.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.48); font-size: 14.5px; }

  .generalize {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .generalize.three {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    max-width: none;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .generalize.three { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .generalize li {
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
  }
  .generalize.three li { font-size: 14px; }
  .generalize li strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
